<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">André Grétry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1741">1741</a>–<a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as André-Ernest-Modeste Grétry</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">André-Ernest-Modeste Grétry was a composer from Liège who made his career in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and became the leading composer of French opéra comique in the late eighteenth century. His works included Zémire et Azor and Richard Cœur-de-lion (1784), and he was honoured by both the old monarchy and the Revolution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andre-gretry.jpg" alt="André Grétry" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Élisabeth Louise Vigée Le Brun · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andr%C3%A9_Ernest_Modeste_Gr%C3%A9try.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
